---
import { Code } from '@astrojs/starlight/components';

import ReferencePage from '../../components/ReferencePage.astro';

const commitGrammar = '[(i/N) ][TICKET] type(scope)!: description';
const hookCommand = 'repo-standards commit-message .git/COMMIT_EDITMSG --fix-safe';
---

<ReferencePage
  title="Git policies"
  description="Commit-message, pull-request history, and pull-request description policies."
>
  <article class="git-policies">
    <header class="section-heading">
      <p class="eyebrow">Version-control contracts</p>
      <h1>Git policies</h1>
      <p class="lede">Commit messages and pull-request history are enforced outside the repository rule registry. They are commands and hooks, so they do not appear in the rule count.</p>
    </header>

    <section class="git-policy-grammar" aria-labelledby="commit-header-grammar">
      <p class="eyebrow">Commit header grammar</p>
      <h2 id="commit-header-grammar"><code>{commitGrammar}</code></h2>
      <p>The optional series marker comes first, followed by an optional ticket, a conventional-commit type, optional scope and breaking marker, then a concise description.</p>
    </section>

    <div class="capability-grid git-policy-grid">
      <section class="capability-card" aria-labelledby="managed-commit-headers">
        <p class="data-line">commit-msg · local and CI</p>
        <h2 id="managed-commit-headers">Managed commit headers</h2>
        <p>The hook validates new commit headers. Safe fixing normalizes only casing and spacing; commit bodies and trailers remain untouched.</p>
        <Code code={hookCommand} lang="bash" frame="terminal" wrap />
        <p>CI validates every direct non-merge commit, including commits created before a contributor installed the hook.</p>
        <p><a href="/cli/#commit-message">Commit-message command reference</a></p>
      </section>

      <section class="capability-card" aria-labelledby="reviewable-pr-history">
        <p class="data-line">pull-request history · CI authoritative</p>
        <h2 id="reviewable-pr-history">Reviewable pull-request history</h2>
        <p>Pull requests may contain up to five direct commits by default. Longer histories must use a complete, ordered <code>(1/N)</code> through <code>(N/N)</code> series.</p>
        <p>The local hook is advisory because it cannot know the final base and head refs. CI evaluates the direct commits in the submitted pull request.</p>
        <p><a href="/cli/#pull-request.commits">Pull-request commit command reference</a></p>
      </section>
    </div>

    <header class="section-heading">
      <p class="eyebrow">Pull-request descriptions</p>
      <h2 id="pull-request-descriptions">Pull-request descriptions</h2>
      <p class="lede">A reviewer should understand the change, its risk, and how to test it without opening the ticket.</p>
    </header>

    <div class="capability-grid git-policy-grid">
      <section class="capability-card" aria-labelledby="required-body-sections">
        <p class="data-line">review policy · CI authoritative</p>
        <h2 id="required-body-sections">Required sections</h2>
        <p>Each repository lists its required headings in <code>pull_request.review_policy.required_body_sections</code>. The review policy fails a pull request when one of them is missing or holds only HTML comments.</p>
        <p>The <a href="/rules/pull-request-template-sections/"><code>repository/documentation/pull-request-template-sections</code></a> rule checks that the pull-request template has every required heading.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="capability-card" aria-labelledby="show-the-change">
        <p class="data-line">picture · own section</p>
        <h2 id="show-the-change">Show the change</h2>
        <p>Put one small picture in its own section, not at the top of the body. Pick the smallest one that makes the point: pseudocode, a call tree, a component tree, a file tree, a Mermaid diagram, or a diff of one of these. Keep only the parts that changed and their direct neighbors.</p>
        <p>For evidence, show before and after. A screenshot is best for a visual change. Next best is a test that failed before the change and passes after it.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="capability-card" aria-labelledby="door-and-qa">
        <p class="data-line">risk · rollback</p>
        <h2 id="door-and-qa">One-way or two-way door</h2>
        <p>A two-way door is a change a revert undoes. A one-way door is a change a revert can't undo, such as deleted data, a migration, a public API change, or a message already sent. Say which one and why. For a one-way door, say how you would recover.</p>
        <p>End the blast-radius section with numbered test steps a QA agent can follow: setup, one action per step, and what the tester should see.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="capability-card" aria-labelledby="writing-style">
        <p class="data-line">prose · plain English</p>
        <h2 id="writing-style">Writing style</h2>
        <p>Use very simple language. Someone new to the team should understand what the change does from the body alone.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Say what changes for the user or the caller before how the code does it: "Publish now shows an error", not "adds a validator".</li>
          <li>Use short sentences with one idea each, in active voice.</li>
          <li>Use plain words: "use", not "leverage".</li>
          <li>Give the number or the mechanism, not a feeling: "p95 drops from 900 ms to 300 ms", not "much faster".</li>
          <li>Cut filler and hedging. Write whole sentences, not arrows or shorthand.</li>
          <li>Don't use em dashes or bold labels that repeat the line.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>
    </div>
  </article>
</ReferencePage>
